+## Developer diagnostics
+
+The homepage includes independent browser-side checks for a hardware device on
+private Wi-Fi and a companion CLI on the reader's own machine. The example
+endpoints are `http://192.168.1.100:8080/health` and
+`http://127.0.0.1:45678/health`; readers can replace the URL, port, and path.
+No device discovery or requests run on page load. Connect performs a read-only
+GET, with a 15-second timeout; successful 2xx responses report HTTP status,
+latency, and the time checked. This is a reachability check, not a persistent
+connection or an implementation of a device/CLI protocol.
+
+Serve the site over HTTPS. Each endpoint must implement a read-only status route
+and return standard CORS headers, such as
+`Access-Control-Allow-Origin: https://your-site.example` (the actual site origin,
+without a path). Requests omit credentials and reject redirects. There is no
+backend relay, so addresses always refer to the reader's network or machine.
+
+The widget queries `local-network` and `loopback-network` permissions separately,
+observes permission changes, and uses `targetAddressSpace: 'local'` or
+`'loopback'` in fetch options. Unrecognized permission queries gracefully fall
+back to an explicit click. Browser support varies; browsers without the needed
+local HTTP exemptions may require HTTPS endpoints with trusted certificates or
+a current browser with Local Network Access support. Permission denial, CORS,
+firewalls, and network errors can look alike to JavaScript; the widget provides
+troubleshooting without claiming to distinguish them. No deprecated Private
+Network Access preflight headers are required.
